  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="disc-content">

      <!-- 1. HOW TO USE THE CALCULATOR -->
      <h2>How to Use the Discount & Sale Price Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        Finding accurate discount amounts, percentage savings, stacked coupon totals, or pre-discount retail values is simple with our comprehensive multi-mode calculator:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Select your calculation mode:</strong> Choose from <em>Sale Price</em> (calculate final tag price with % or fixed cash markdown), <em>Discount %</em> (calculate rate off from sale price), <em>Original Price</em> (restore MSRP tag price), <em>Stacked (Multi)</em> (sequential promotional discounts), or <em>Buy X Get Y (BOGO)</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Select your preferred currency:</strong> Choose between $, ₹, €, £, ¥, ₩, R$, or custom symbols.</li>
        <li><strong>Enter the price and discount values:</strong> Enter the retail price, discount percentage (or cash markdown), and item quantity.</li>
        <li><strong>Enable sales tax / VAT if desired:</strong> Toggle the tax option and specify your local sales tax percentage to calculate the final checkout total.</li>
        <li><strong>Click Calculate Discount:</strong> Review the itemized savings, checkout total, visual paid/saved bar, and mathematical breakdown.</li>
        <li><strong>Download PDF Report or Save:</strong> Export a clean branded PDF summary or save your calculation for shopping budgets.</li>
      </ol>

      <!-- 2. HOW TO CALCULATE A DISCOUNT -->
      <h2>How to Calculate a Discount</h2>
      <p>
        Calculating a standard commercial percentage discount involves two arithmetic steps:
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>Discount Amount = Original Price × (Discount Percentage ÷ 100)</code><br />
        <code>Sale Price = Original Price − Discount Amount</code>
      </p>
      <p>
        For instance, if a designer jacket originally retails at <strong>$500.00</strong> with a <strong>20% discount</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>Discount Amount = $500 × (20 ÷ 100) = $100.00 Saved</code></li>
        <li><code>Final Sale Price = $500 − $100 = $400.00</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 italic">
        * Example shown before state or local sales taxes.
      </p>

      <!-- 3. HOW TO CALCULATE DISCOUNT PERCENTAGE -->
      <h2>How to Calculate Discount Percentage</h2>
      <p>
        When you know the original tag price and the discounted price on a receipt, you can determine the exact percentage saved using the reverse discount formula:
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>Discount Percentage = ((Original Price − Sale Price) ÷ Original Price) × 100</code>
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, if a smartphone marked down from $800.00 is sold for $600.00:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>Money Saved = $800 − $600 = $200.00</code></li>
        <li><code>Discount % = ($200 ÷ $800) × 100 = 25.0% Off</code></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- 4. HOW MULTIPLE DISCOUNTS WORK -->
      <h2>How Multiple (Stacked) Discounts Work</h2>
      <p>
        A frequent misconception in retail shopping is that stacked promotional discounts add together linearly. For example, a <strong>20% storewide clearance</strong> combined with an <strong>extra 10% loyalty coupon</strong> does not equal a 30% discount.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sequential discounts are calculated compounding off the declining intermediate subtotal:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Original Price:</strong> $1,000.00</li>
        <li><strong>Step 1 (20% Clearance):</strong> $1,000 − $200 = $800.00</li>
        <li><strong>Step 2 (Extra 10% Coupon):</strong> $800 − (10% of $800) = $800 − $80 = <strong>$720.00</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Total Money Saved:</strong> $280.00</li>
        <li><strong>Effective Compounded Discount:</strong> <code>($280 ÷ $1,000) × 100 = 28.0%</code> (not 30.0%)</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- 5. BUY X GET Y (BOGO) EXPLAINED -->
      <h2>Buy X Get Y (BOGO) Bundle Arithmetic</h2>
      <p>
        "Buy 1 Get 1 Free" or "Buy 2 Get 1 at 50% Off" offers have different effective discount percentages depending on bundle sizes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Buy 1 Get 1 Free (BOGO):</strong> 2 items total, 1 paid at full price. Effective discount = <strong>50.0% off</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Buy 1 Get 1 at 50% Off:</strong> 2 items total. Item 1 = 100%, Item 2 = 50%. Total = 150% for 2 items. Effective discount = <strong>25.0% off</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Buy 2 Get 1 Free:</strong> 3 items total, 2 paid at full price. Effective discount = <code>(1 ÷ 3) × 100 = 33.33% off</code>.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- 6. SALES TAX AFTER DISCOUNT -->
      <h2>Is Sales Tax Calculated Before or After a Discount?</h2>
      <p>
        In most jurisdictions (including US states and European VAT systems), statutory sales tax is applied to the <strong>net discounted sale price</strong> paid by the customer, rather than the original manufacturer MSRP list price.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, if you use a manufacturer rebate or cash voucher that is reimbursed to the retailer by a third party, tax may be assessed on the pre-rebate subtotal depending on regional statutes.
      </p>

      <!-- 7. PRACTICAL EXAMPLES -->
      <h2>Discount & Sale Price Examples</h2>

      <h3>Example 1: Fixed Markdown</h3>
      <p>
        An appliance listed for $450.00 has an instant cash voucher of $75.00. The sale price is $450 − $75 = <strong>$375.00</strong>, representing an effective 16.67% markdown.
      </p>

      <h3>Example 2: Reverse MSRP Restoration</h3>
      <p>
        A watch bought on Black Friday for $240.00 was sold at 40% off. The original price before discount was $240 ÷ (1 − 0.40) = <strong>$400.00</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- 8. IMPORTANT INFORMATION AND DISCLAIMER -->
      <h2>Important Information & Disclaimers</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Rounding Discrepancies:</strong> Commercial point-of-sale registers round fractional cents at each item line, which may produce minor penny variations.</li>
        <li><strong>Merchant Coupon Terms:</strong> Retailers may apply coupons, rewards points, and promotional codes in differing sequences according to their specific checkout terms.</li>
        <li><strong>Educational Reference:</strong> This calculator provides mathematical estimates for personal shopping, budgeting, and commercial reference.</li>
      </ul>

    </section>
